</mj-text>
    <mj-text padding="10px 12px" font-family="TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif;" color="#000000">
  <h2 style="font-size: 24px; line-height: 1.2; font-weight: 400; margin-top: 2px !important;">
    America’s shield strain
  </h2>
</mj-text>
<mj-image
  border-radius="10px"
  padding="10px 12px"
  width="600px"
  src="https://www.presidentialsummary.com/email/images/REPLACE_ME.jpg"
  alt="America’s shield strain"
  href="https://www.presidentialsummary.com/"
/>
<mj-text padding="10px 12px" font-family="Roboto+Serif" color="#000000">
  <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5"><strong>What happened?</strong> The US Congressional Budget Office (CBO) says President Trump’s proposed “Golden Dome” missile defense system <a href="https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cqxp27j1xyjo" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none;
                border-bottom: 2px solid #4d3060;
                color: black;">could cost about $1.2T over 20 years</a>, far above the earlier $175B estimate. The system is designed to protect the US from ballistic, cruise, and next-generation missile threats using land, sea, and space-based technology. However, the CBO warned it may still be overwhelmed by a major attack from Russia or China. <strong>  </strong></p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5"><strong>Why? </strong>The Golden Dome may struggle against Russia or China because both have large, advanced missile arsenals designed to overwhelm defense systems. A full-scale attack could involve countless ballistic missiles, cruise missiles, hypersonic weapons, decoys, and drones launched from different directions. Even a highly advanced shield may not track, target, and intercept every threat in time. Space-based sensors and interceptors could also become targets themselves, especially in a conflict with a major power.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5"><strong>Meanwhile: </strong>The US military campaign against Iran has costed an estimated $29B, according to a senior Pentagon official. That marks a $4B jump from the estimate given weeks earlier, raising fresh questions over the monetary burden of the conflict. The updated figure comes as Democrats try to make the war’s rising cost a midterm election issue, linking it to inflation, household expenses, and pressure on American taxpayers.</p>
</mj-text>
  </mj-column>
</mj-section>

