<mj-section
  background-color="#eff1f4"
  css-class="border-line"
  padding="1px 0.5px 1px 1px"
  border-radius="5px"
>
  <mj-column background-color="#fff" border-radius="5px" padding="0px">
    <mj-spacer height="10px" />
    <mj-text padding="10px 12px" font-family="TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif;" color="#000000">
  <h2 style="font-size: 24px; line-height: 1.2; font-weight: 400; margin-top: 2px !important;">
    Beyond ovarian cysts
  </h2>
</mj-text>
<mj-image
  border-radius="10px"
  padding="10px 12px"
  width="600px"
  src="https://www.presidentialsummary.com/email/images/REPLACE_ME.jpg"
  alt="Beyond ovarian cysts"
  href="https://www.presidentialsummary.com/"
/>
<mj-text padding="10px 12px" font-family="Roboto+Serif" color="#000000">
  <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5"><strong>What happened? </strong>PCOS, a hormonal disorder affecting around 1 in 8 women, has been renamed to <a href="https://www.livescience.com/health/reproductive-health/the-name-was-inaccurate-pcos-gets-a-new-name-after-years-long-effort" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none;
                border-bottom: 2px solid #4d3060;
                color: black;">polyendocrine metabolic ovarian syndrome</a>, or PMOS. The renaming followed a global consultation involving patients, healthcare professionals, and 56 academic, clinical, and patient organizations. Experts hope the change will reduce confusion, improve diagnosis, and shift treatment beyond just gynecological symptoms.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5"><strong>Why?</strong> “Polycystic” suggested that people with the condition have abnormal ovarian cysts, but researchers say many instead have arrested follicles, which are eggs that fail to fully mature because of hormonal effects. The old name also focused too heavily on ovaries, making the condition seem mainly gynecological. In reality, it affects multiple hormones, metabolism, weight, skin, reproduction, and mental health.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5"><strong>Big picture:</strong> The renaming reflects a broader shift in how the condition is understood, diagnosed, and treated. Experts hope PMOS will push doctors, researchers, and patients to see it as a whole-body endocrine and metabolic condition. The change may also reduce stigma, improve awareness, support earlier diagnosis, and guide future updates to ultrasound and clinical language.</p>
</mj-text>
  </mj-column>
</mj-section>
    <mj-spacer height="10px" />
    <mj-section
      background-color="#eff1f4"
      css-class="border-line"
